      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Merrin & Sallow Architects, a fictional London architecture practice. First you're on the red team: you don't go after one person, you go after the whole practice at once. Gather the staff and how their email addresses are built, build a word list from the practice's own website, then try one likely password against every account, and a stolen breach list across many services. Then it's your own seat: the alerts arrive, and you tell the real from the fake. Only that part is scored.",
        "stages": {
          "osint": {
            "text": "Open the browser and read the practice's public pages. Click a line to add it to your notes; the useful lines are the patterns and projects, not individual staff. Five useful facts are there to find, and the more you note, the more the campaign has to work with."
          }
        },
        "flipHeading": "Now it's your inbox.",
        "flipGuessIntro": "You watched one password open the predictable accounts across the whole practice, and a stolen one open the accounts where it had been reused. Here is the other end: your own seat, with the alerts starting to arrive.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "One password off the website, tried once across everyone, opened the predictable accounts. No clever cracking, just reuse and words anyone could read. A different password for every account, and not a word off the website, is what would have stopped it. Now the alerts are arriving, and some are fakes.",
        "flipNoSend": "Nothing of the practice's was opened to replay, but the lesson holds: a reused password, or a word off the website, is what opens an account. Next, you tell the real alerts from the fakes.",
        "flipMissed": "The attack opened nothing this time, but a reused password or a company word is still what opens accounts at scale. Next, you tell the real alerts from the fakes.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing landed this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It opened the predictable accounts with nothing clever at all, just reuse and words off the website. Next, you tell the real alerts from the fakes.",
        "noBlameLine": "Reusing a password, or using a word off the website, is ordinary and common; this is how accounts are opened at scale, not a sign anyone was careless. A miss here just shows what to change tonight.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every fake was caught, with every sign named.",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these rather than acting on them, you kept a fake away from the rest of your team.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday, three things at home: switch on a password manager and let it make a different password for every account, starting with your email; check your personal email address on a breach-checking site (the NCSC suggests Have I Been Pwned); and turn on 2-step verification, or a passkey, for your email first. Report anything at work using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These alerts follow an attack on the whole practice, and anyone can be caught out by a convincing fake. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "Password spraying",
            "text": "One likely password, tried just once against every account, so no single account locks. The accounts that open are the predictable ones: a company word, a project name, a season and a year."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Credential stuffing",
            "text": "A list of address and password pairs stolen from one website, tried automatically across many others. Where someone reused that password at work, the attacker is straight in."
          },
          {
            "heading": "The one habit",
            "text": "A different password for every account, not a word off the website, kept in a manager, breaks both: the spray has nothing predictable to try, and a stolen pair opens nothing else."
          },
          {
            "heading": "What the practice does",
            "text": "On the practice's side: accounts lock after a run of bad sign-ins, MFA is on everything, the obvious words are blocked from passwords, and a monitoring partner watches the breach lists for the practice's addresses. These are set up for you; you don't have to switch them on."
          }
        ]
      },
